        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Akiachak%2C_Alaska"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Akiachak sits on the west bank of the Kuskokwim River in the Yukon–Kuskokwim Delta, 18 miles northeast of Bethel.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Akiachak%2C_Alaska"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The village's climate sees about 16 inches of rain and 50 inches of snow each year, with summer highs between 42 and 62°F.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10,
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Akiachak%2C_Alaska"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "In 1987, Akiachak became the first Alaskan community to dissolve its municipal city government, shifting to a traditional village council.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4,
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.kupi.com/en/explore/united-states/akiachak"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Akiachak was the lead plaintiff in the landmark lawsuit Akiachak Native Community v., setting a legal precedent for tribal governance.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.kupi.com/en/explore/united-states/akiachak"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "The Yup'ik peoples historically used the Akiachak region as a seasonal subsistence site, relying on fishing and hunting.",
